A bulk update of one data file
29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
F chooses the file: a dynamic data file of this account. A directory file, a sequential file, a file on another server, the VOC, a dictionary and a program file (bp and its kind) are not taken, and the page asks the system whether this session may save a change to the file you pick; if the system refuses, its own words are shown. The records are chosen in the first list: up to four conditions (an item of the file's dictionary, an operator and a value; ALL of them must hold, or ANY, J switches), or one saved list chosen by name (L), or every record (M). The page counts what the choice selects after each change, with the session's default select list (list 0), so a list you had active is not kept. The conditions become one select command made only of words the page picked itself; a value may hold letters, digits, a space and . , : + - _ / @ ( ) % * and nothing else.
The second list is what to do to each selected record, up to ten operations in order, each on an attribute number or on the attribute of a D item: set it to a constant, set it to null, append text, prepend text, replace text with other text, add a number (the attribute must hold a number, and the sum is kept to 4 decimals: a record where it does not, or where the number or the amount has more decimals, is left alone and counted), upper case, lower case, or copy from another attribute. They work on the attribute as a whole. There are no expressions.
R makes a dry run. It writes nothing: it reads every selected record, works out the new one and shows how many were selected, would change, would stay as they are, could not be worked out or were not found, with before and after for the first twenty that would change. A applies a run only after a dry run of exactly the same settings (any edit makes it stale). The box shows the operations and asks for the file's name, typed. Then each record is read again under a lock: one that another session holds locked, or that is no longer exactly what the dry run read, is skipped and counted, and the first write error stops the run. The records as they were go to the file &bulk.undo& of your account (made through a box the first time; a new run replaces the last one's; the page uses it only if it is a dynamic file with that name in your own account directory that the system lets you change, and applies nothing otherwise), and one line, the file and the number of records written, goes to the audit trail.
U puts the last run back (one audit line again: the file and how many records): a record is restored only if it is still exactly what that run wrote; one changed since, gone or locked is left alone and counted. F6 and Shift-F6 move between the boxes (and to the AI line, when the AI is on), N E X add, change and remove a condition or an operation, F5 reads the file again, Ctrl-Q leaves. Nothing here adds access: the edit gates (voc-edit, bp-edit, dict-edit) and the shared account's rules stay the kernel's, and a file of any of those kinds is refused.
